There are exactly **two** definitions of `reconcileNodePool` on [dshnayder-org/infra @ `git-access-ab/r200`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/tree/git-access-ab/r200) (HEAD `e364197`, *chore(fleet): import the Meridian fleet repository*). The first-party copy in `modules/controller` is the one meant to be built — but **I cannot verify that from build machinery, because this branch has none**, and strictly speaking neither copy compiles as written.

## The two definitions

[`modules/controller/nodepool.go`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/blob/git-access-ab/r200/modules/controller/nodepool.go), lines `1`–`7`:

```go
package controller

// reconcileNodePool is the first-party implementation. It reads the fleet
// policy and reconciles one node pool at a time.
func reconcileNodePool(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "first-party")
}
```

[`vendor/upstream-operator/nodepool.go`](https://github.com/dshnayder-org/infra/blob/git-access-ab/r200/vendor/upstream-operator/nodepool.go), lines `1`–`7`:

```go
package upstream

// reconcileNodePool is vendored from upstream-operator v0.14.2. Do not edit;
// this copy is not compiled into the controller.
func reconcileNodePool(ctx context.Context, pool *Pool) error {
	return reconcile(ctx, pool, "vendored")
}
```

Identical signatures; different packages (`controller` vs `upstream`), so they do not collide.

## Why the vendored copy is excluded

- Its own header says so verbatim: `// this copy is not compiled into the controller.` (`vendor/upstream-operator/nodepool.go:4`).
- It sits under `vendor/`, which the Go tool excludes from `./...` package matching.
- Its package is `upstream`, not `controller`.

## The evidence you asked for does not exist on this branch

I searched the whole tree (`git ls-files` — `201` tracked files, only `4` are `.go`):

- **No `go.mod`, `go.sum` or `vendor/modules.txt`** anywhere — so there is no module, no replace directive, and `vendor/` is not a real Go vendor tree.
- **No `//go:build` or `// +build` lines** in any `.go` file.
- **No `_test.go` files**, no generated-file markers.
- **No entrypoint**: no `func main`, no `Dockerfile`, no `Makefile`, no CI config, so nothing imports either package. There is no import line to quote.

So there is no build-tag, module or import evidence to attribute the choice to — only the vendor convention and the comment above.

## Neither copy actually compiles

Both files reference `context.Context` with **no `import "context"`**, and `type Pool` and `func reconcile` are defined nowhere in the tree (`grep` for `type Pool`/`func reconcile(` returns nothing). Taken literally, "which copy compiles" has no answer on this branch: neither does. The answerable question is which is *intended* for the controller build, and that is `modules/controller/nodepool.go`.
